  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-argument essay (with a wry, Terminator-framed tone) aimed at engineering and security teams skeptical of \"agentic AI threat\" hype. Core thesis: no single AI breakthrough is coming; instead, two ordinary compounding curves multiply — agent *endurance* (METR data showing task length doubling every ~4–7 months) and orchestration *breadth* (citing Anthropic's GTG-1002 disruption, where Claude Code coordinated ~30 sub-agent targets) — producing a threat that scales as duration × breadth while human security review scales only linearly. The economic twist is \"LLMjacking\" (Sysdig): attackers with stolen cloud credentials run agent workloads on the victim's meter, severing the industry's assumption that the capability user is the payer, so price/access gates filter buyers but not tenants. Recurring framing: the Terminator quote \"absolutely will not stop, ever\" reinterpreted as \"will not stop *billing*,\" plus the swarm argument that cheap automated attempts (1 − (1−p)^n) only need to succeed once.",
